"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,785\nThe basal ganglia: habits and skills\n\n3\n00:00:09,785 --> 00:00:12,405\nIn 1996, Barbara Knowlton,\n\n4\n00:00:12,607 --> 00:00:16,738\nJennifer Mangels and Larry Squire gave people a weather forecasting game.\n\n5\n00:00:17,347 --> 00:00:20,063\nCards with shapes predicted rain or sun,\n\n6\n00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:21,704\nbut only some of the time,\n\n7\n00:00:21,724 --> 00:00:23,803\nso the game had to be learned slowly,\n\n8\n00:00:24,018 --> 00:00:25,393\ntrial by trial.\n\n9\n00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,431\nPeople with amnesia learned the game normally,\n\n10\n00:00:28,707 --> 00:00:30,453\nbut could not remember the training.\n\n11\n00:00:30,952 --> 00:00:34,085\nPeople with Parkinson's disease remembered the training,\n\n12\n00:00:34,162 --> 00:00:35,540\nbut did not learn the game.\n\n13\n00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:38,814\nParkinson's disease affects the basal ganglia,\n\n14\n00:00:39,004 --> 00:00:41,231\na group of structures deep in the brain.\n\n15\n00:00:41,754 --> 00:00:44,107\nTheir largest part, the striatum,\n\n16\n00:00:44,165 --> 00:00:46,605\ndepends on the brain chemical dopamine.\n\n17\n00:00:47,081 --> 00:00:51,334\nIn Parkinson's, the midbrain cells that supply it are lost.\n\n18\n00:00:54,004 --> 00:00:56,584\nThe basal ganglia learn by repetition.\n\n19\n00:00:57,084 --> 00:00:58,739\nIn rats learning a maze,\n\n20\n00:00:58,935 --> 00:01:02,835\ncells in the striatum first fired all the way through each run.\n\n21\n00:01:03,371 --> 00:01:05,086\nAs the route became a habit,\n\n22\n00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:07,919\nthey fired mainly at the start and the end,\n\n23\n00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:10,821\nas if the whole run had become one chunk.\n\n24\n00:01:13,491 --> 00:01:16,135\nA habit is an action triggered by a cue,\n\n25\n00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:17,233\nwithout much thought.\n\n26\n00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:20,892\nThat frees the prefrontal workbench for other things.\n\n27\n00:01:21,195 --> 00:01:23,520\nIt also makes habits hard to stop,\n\n28\n00:01:23,793 --> 00:01:25,415\neven when they no longer help.\n\n29\n00:01:27,585 --> 00:01:29,152\nHow long does a habit take?\n\n30\n00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:31,338\nIn a 2010 study,\n\n31\n00:01:31,406 --> 00:01:33,875\npeople repeated a new daily behavior,\n\n32\n00:01:33,938 --> 00:01:35,782\nlike eating fruit with lunch.\n\n33\n00:01:36,191 --> 00:01:37,790\nFor those who formed a habit,\n\n34\n00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,449\nthe typical time was 66 days,\n\n35\n00:01:40,682 --> 00:01:43,521\nranging from 18 to 254.\n\n36\n00:01:43,963 --> 00:01:46,455\nMissing a single day did not matter much.\n\n37\n00:01:48,625 --> 00:01:50,347\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n38\n00:01:50,694 --> 00:01:56,351\nSkills like reading, typing or solving equations become automatic with practice.\n\n39\n00:01:56,728 --> 00:01:58,906\nRepeat them often, in the same setting,\n\n40\n00:01:58,926 --> 00:01:59,870\nwith feedback.\n\n41\n00:02:00,377 --> 00:02:02,419\nOnce the basics run on their own,\n\n42\n00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:04,795\nyour mind is free for harder thinking.\n\n43\n00:02:06,965 --> 00:02:09,641\nThe basal ganglia turn practice into habit.\n\n44\n00:02:10,018 --> 00:02:12,513\nWhat you repeat, they make automatic.\n\n"
